data.format:
One JPG per roster row, 2,646 px wide, 85–141 px high and 70–110 KB. Two context images go with every request: `context/roster_header.jpg` (2,646×194 px) and `context/icons.jpg` (1,279×912 px). The file `context/shared_context_prompt.txt` also lists icon names but is not sent; the allowed names reach the model through the `icon` enumeration in `dataclass.py`. The ground truth is one JSON file per row, following the `Schedule` dataclass in `dataclass.py`:

```json
{
  "year": 2025,
  "month": "April",
  "persons": [
    {
      "id": "103",
      "profession": "WBL",
      "employment_percent": 80,
      "is_jumper": false,
      "days": [
        {
          "date": "2025-04-01",
          "shifts": [
            {
              "icon": "F-Dienst 1",
              "length": "full",
              "planned_on_current_unit": true,
              "alternate_unit": "LE"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "date": "2025-04-11",
          "shifts": [
            {
              "icon": "HomeOffice",
              "length": "half_left",
              "planned_on_current_unit": true,
              "alternate_unit": null
            },
            {
              "icon": "Freier Tag",
              "length": "half_right",
              "planned_on_current_unit": true,
              "alternate_unit": null
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
```

- `id`: the staff number written in the row's margin
- `profession`: the section label printed above the row (e.g. `WBL`, `SRK`, `Tertiär`)
- `employment_percent`, `is_jumper`: the employment percentage and the `S` (jumper) column
- `icon`: the legend name of the cell's icon, or `null` for an empty cell
- `length`: `full`, `half_left` or `half_right`; a split cell gives two entries for the same date
- `planned_on_current_unit`: `false` if the icon has red shading or a red border
- `alternate_unit`: the unit abbreviation below the cell (e.g. `LE`, `TS`), several joined with a comma, or `null`

Every row covers all 30 days of April; the 21 rows hold 655 shifts.
